# Network security

## Transport

- **HTTPS only, everywhere.** No `http://` endpoints in any environment, including staging.
- Enforce at the platform layer: iOS App Transport Security stays enabled with no
  broad `NSAllowsArbitraryLoads` exception; Android `networkSecurityConfig` sets
  `cleartextTrafficPermitted="false"`.
- Minimum TLS 1.2; prefer TLS 1.3 where the backend supports it.

## Certificate pinning

- Pin the backend's certificate or public key (SPKI hash) for production API hosts using a
  vetted pinning library or the platform network-security config.
- Ship **backup pins** for the next key rotation, and define the rotation runbook before
  enabling pinning — a bricked app is the failure mode of unplanned rotation.
- Pin-validation failure **fails closed**: no silent fallback to unpinned connections.
- Debug builds may relax pinning for local proxying; the relaxation is compile-time gated
  and impossible in release builds.

## Single HTTP client

- All HTTP goes through the shared client module (`src/lib/httpClient.ts`). Raw `fetch` /
  `axios` calls scattered in product code are forbidden — the client owns:
  - Base URL per environment (never user-controllable).
  - Auth-header injection and token refresh (see `security/04-authentication.mdc`).
  - Timeouts and bounded retries with backoff (no infinite retry storms).
  - Consistent error normalization — server error bodies are never rendered raw to users.
- WebSockets go through a single owned client with the same auth and reconnect discipline.

## API hygiene

- Validate response shapes at the boundary (mappers narrow `unknown` → typed models);
  a malformed response degrades to a typed fallback, never a crash or a trusted value.
- Never build URLs by concatenating unvalidated user input; encode all path/query params.
- Idempotency keys on mutating requests that may be retried.
- No sensitive data in query strings (they leak into logs and proxies) — use headers or
  request bodies.

## Environments

- Environment endpoints and keys live in per-environment config files consumed at build
  time; the production app cannot be pointed at arbitrary hosts at runtime.
- Any in-app environment switcher is `__DEV__`-only.
